WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.794
Safe Rust prevents many buffer-overflow
classes,

2
00:00:02.874 --> 00:00:05.649
but unsafe code and foreign interfaces
require special care.

3
00:00:05.729 --> 00:00:08.770
Raw pointers, external C APIs, platform
calls, device interfaces,

4
00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:12.691
and custom low-level abstractions may not
carry Rust's checked reference and bounds

5
00:00:12.771 --> 00:00:13.411
information.

6
00:00:13.491 --> 00:00:16.372
At those boundaries, human review and
safety contracts matter.

7
00:00:16.452 --> 00:00:19.120
Review starts with pointer validity and
buffer contracts.

8
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.961
Is the pointer non-null when required?

9
00:00:21.041 --> 00:00:22.908
Is it aligned for the type being accessed?

10
00:00:22.988 --> 00:00:24.215
Is the memory initialized?

11
00:00:24.295 --> 00:00:25.949
How many bytes or elements are valid?

12
00:00:26.029 --> 00:00:28.216
Can the external function write into the
buffer?

13
00:00:28.296 --> 00:00:30.751
Does the Rust side reserve enough space
for that write?

14
00:00:30.831 --> 00:00:34.245
Ownership transfer and allocation
responsibility are equally important.

15
00:00:34.325 --> 00:00:35.499
Who allocated the memory?

16
00:00:35.579 --> 00:00:36.699
Who is allowed to free it?

17
00:00:36.779 --> 00:00:38.113
Which allocator must be used?

18
00:00:38.193 --> 00:00:40.807
Can external code retain a pointer after
the call returns?

19
00:00:40.887 --> 00:00:44.089
Can Rust safely access the buffer after
handing it across the boundary?

20
00:00:44.169 --> 00:00:46.516
These questions should be documented, not
guessed.

21
00:00:46.596 --> 00:00:49.050
Unsafe code should be small, justified,
and isolated.

22
00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:52.652
A short unsafe block with a clear safety
comment is easier to review than a broad

23
00:00:52.732 --> 00:00:55.453
module where assumptions are spread across
many functions.

24
00:00:55.533 --> 00:00:58.254
The comment should explain why the buffer
contract is valid,

25
00:00:58.334 --> 00:01:01.321
what has been checked, and what future
maintainers must preserve.

26
00:01:01.401 --> 00:01:04.229
A safe wrapper should protect callers from
invalid buffer use.

27
00:01:04.309 --> 00:01:07.564
Ordinary safe code should not need to
remember raw pointer length rules,

28
00:01:07.644 --> 00:01:10.258
allocation pairing, or callback lifetime
requirements.

29
00:01:10.338 --> 00:01:14.019
The wrapper should encode those rules in
types and runtime checks where possible,

30
00:01:14.099 --> 00:01:16.340
and document the remaining assumptions
clearly.
